  "findings": "Target activity identified from the synchronization checks as A_Accepted, which shows disproportionately high synchronization time for Application + Case_R: median 72902.0 seconds, mean 122937.87 seconds, std 235905.94 seconds, count 31498. This is much higher than A_Complete for the same object types, where median synchronization is only 264.0 seconds. Evidence-supported causal chain: applications are linked to downstream Offer objects and Workflow objects; Application->Offer has 42995 O2O links and Application->Workflow has 31509 O2O links. Offer creation/branching varies per application: mean 1.36 offers, median 1.0, max 10. More offers are positively correlated with longer Application flow time: Pearson r=0.2202, Spearman rho=0.1782, p=0.0. Offer-side processing contains very long delays, especially O_Sent (mail and online) with median sojourn 941202.0 seconds and O_Sent (online only) with median sojourn 414284.0 seconds, plus long waits before offer outcomes such as O_Cancelled median waiting 2635680.0 seconds, O_Returned 609943.0 seconds, O_Refused 423518.0 seconds, and O_Accepted 351415.5 seconds. Workflow-side processing also shows high mean delays, especially W_Call after offers with mean sojourn 228442.59 seconds and mean waiting 217077.48 seconds, although its median synchronization for Case_R + Workflow is 0.0 seconds. At A_Accepted, the event always requires co-presence of Application and Case_R, with exactly one object of each type per event. The likely causal mechanism is therefore: upstream branching into one or more offers and associated workflow handling creates heterogeneous completion times; the final acceptance activity requires the Application and Case_R states to converge; when one side is ready earlier than the other, synchronization delay accumulates before A_Accepted. Evidence-supported links are the high A_Accepted synchronization time, mandatory Application + Case_R co-presence, variable offer counts, positive offer-count/flow-time correlation, and long offer/workflow delays. Hypotheses are that offer-side outcome waiting and post-offer workflow handling are the concrete upstream blockers causing the Case_R/Application misalignment at A_Accepted, because the available tools show association and timing patterns but do not directly prove event-level causality into A_Accepted.",
